{"filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/defect_cocycle_with_findings.tex","newString":"\\paragraph{Immediate follow-ups.}\n(1)~The residue-only uniform gap is now computed over the full group to $Q=3^7$\nand stabilizes; the open structural question is the killed-height\n\\emph{quadratic-character} degeneracy ($\\kappa_Q=0$): rebuild the height\ncoordinate \\emph{coupled} to the character (or prove the quadratic mode is a\ngenuine height-transport artifact) so the killed operator inherits the residue\ngap. (2)~Switch the defect-window","oldString":"\\paragraph{Immediate follow-ups.}\n(1)~The killed-height solver is now a converged Arnoldi; extend $\\kappa_Q$ to the\n\\emph{full} character group (not the first four) and push $Q$ higher to test\nwhether $\\kappa_Q\\gtrsim1/\\mathrm{polylog}(Q)$ survives as $Q\\to\\infty$ (the\nfiniteness leak). (2)~Switch the defect-window","originalFile":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\n\\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}\n\\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb,amsthm,mathtools}\n\\usepackage{mathrsfs}\n\\usepackage{bm}\n\\usepackage{enumitem}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage[dvipsnames]{xcolor}\n\\usepackage[most]{tcolorbox}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\\usepackage[hidelinks]{hyperref}\n\\usepackage[nameinlink]{cleveref}\n\n\\allowdisplaybreaks\n\\setlength{\\parskip}{0.4em}\n\n%==================== macros ====================\n\\newcommand{\\E}{\\mathbb{E}}\n\\newcommand{\\C}{\\mathbb{C}}\n\\newcommand{\\R}{\\mathbb{R}}\n\\newcommand{\\Z}{\\mathbb{Z}}\n\\newcommand{\\Xcal}{\\mathcal{X}}\n\\newcommand{\\Lcal}{\\mathcal{L}}\n\\newcommand{\\Ccal}{\\mathscr{C}}\n\\newcommand{\\Om}{\\Omega}\n\\newcommand{\\Qcoll}{\\mathfrak{Q}_{\\mathrm{Coll}}}\n\\newcommand{\\Dcoll}{\\mathfrak{D}_{\\mathrm{Coll}}}\n\\newcommand{\\rhoeff}{\\rho_{\\mathrm{eff}}}\n\\newcommand{\\que}{\\mathrm{que}}\n\\DeclareMathOperator{\\tr}{tr}\n\\DeclareMathOperator{\\hol}{hol}\n\\newcommand{\\KL}[2]{D\\!\\left(#1\\,\\middle\\|\\,#2\\right)}\n\\newcommand{\\epi}[1]{{\\small\\sffamily\\textbf{[#1]}}}\n\n% prose key-statement box\n\\newtcolorbox{keystatement}{%\n  enhanced, sharp corners, boxrule=0.6pt,\n  colback=black!4, colframe=black!70,\n  left=8pt, right=8pt, top=6pt, bottom=6pt,\n  before skip=8pt, after skip=8pt}\n\n% titled box for the central conjectural principle\n\\newtcolorbox{principlebox}[1]{%\n  enhanced, sharp corners, boxrule=0.8pt,\n  colback=black!3, colframe=black!75,\n  coltitle=black, fonttitle=\\bfseries,\n  title={#1},\n  left=8pt, right=8pt, top=6pt, bottom=6pt,\n  before skip=10pt, after skip=10pt}\n\n% box for an empirical finding\n\\newtcolorbox{findingbox}[1]{%\n  enhanced, sharp corners, boxrule=0.6pt,\n  colback=Green!4, colframe=Green!45!black,\n  coltitle=black, fonttitle=\\bfseries,\n  title={#1},\n  left=8pt, right=8pt, top=6pt, bottom=6pt,\n  before skip=8pt, after skip=8pt}\n\n\\theoremstyle{remark}\n\\newtheorem*{remark}{Remark}\n\\theoremstyle{definition}\n\\newtheorem{lemma}{Lemma}\n\n%==================== title ====================\n\\title{A Deterministic Defect Cocycle over the Quenched\\\\\nResidue--Height Spectral Shadow:\\\\\n\\large Theory and a Numerical Test Program}\n\\author{}\n\\date{June 9, 2026}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nThis document merges the theoretical note proposing the deterministic defect\ncocycle $\\Dcoll$ for the accelerated Collatz/Syracuse map with the results of a\ndedicated C numerical test suite built to probe it. Part~I states the object and\nthe conjectural \\emph{Quenched Inverse Principle} unchanged in substance from the\noriginal note, with the corrections suggested in review folded in as remarks.\nParts~II--IV report the suite: its construction, the per-experiment findings, and\na dedicated analysis relating the empirically detected ``shadow'' windows to the\nfinite transfer-operator gaps. The headline outcomes are (i) the load-bearing\n$\\bmod\\,3^r$ congruence identity is verified to $\\sim\\!2.4\\times10^{7}$ checks;\n(ii) the finite character-sector gap $\\lvert\\hat p(\\xi)\\rvert<1$ is realized, but\nthe \\emph{exact} coupled operator departs materially from the i.i.d.\\ Fourier\nshadow; and (iii) single-orbit shadows correlate with the operator gap only at\nthe level of the tilt (they concentrate at the critical tilt $s=1$, where the gap\nis weakest), while the per-sector signal is confounded by finite residue-class\ncount---a concrete instance of the ``circularity leak.'' Nothing here proves\nanything about Collatz; the purpose is to decide whether $\\Dcoll$ is the right\nfinite detector and where it sees the obstruction most clearly.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\begin{remark}[Status]\nPart~I builds on the pro-object $\\Qcoll$ of the previous note (the quenched\nresidue--height spectral shadow); project-internal terms (\\textsf{H23a}, the QSD\nmachinery, the residue spaces $\\Om_Q$, the tilted operators\n$\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}$) are carried over without restatement. The\n\\emph{Quenched Inverse Principle} is conjectural. Throughout, $e(t)=e^{2\\pi i t}$.\nEmpirical numbers in Parts~III--IV come from the suite \\texttt{collatz\\_tests}\n(builds \\textsf{FAST128}/\\textsf{GMP}); every figure is reproducible from the\nrecorded run.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\tableofcontents\n\n\\part{The object and the principle}\n\n\\section{The obstacle that fixes the shape of the object}\n\nA single integer $x_0$ gives one deterministic valuation word\n$w(x_0)=(b_0,b_1,b_2,\\dots)$, where $b_i=v_2(3x_i+1)$. The annealed law is the\npushforward of Haar measure on $\\Z_2$ under $x\\mapsto v_2(3x+1)$. So ``the orbit\nobeys the annealed law'' means: the one prescribed word $w(x_0)$ equidistributes\nfor the residue--height skew product. There is no ergodic theorem for a single\nprescribed non-generic point, and there cannot be: ergodicity yields\nalmost-everywhere statements, which are silent about any individual orbit.\n\nThis dictates the shape of the missing object. It must be a \\emph{dichotomy with\na closure}, not a measure-theoretic average: a pathwise quantity forced either to\ndecay (the orbit equidistributes) or to localize as a finite, excludable\nobstruction. The quantity must reduce, at the trivial character and after\nensemble averaging, to the annealed pressure $M(s)$ and the Cram\\'er tilt; and its\nlocalization must land inside a fixed finite character sector, where\n\\textsf{H23a} can act.\n\n\\section{The object: a deterministic defect cocycle}\n\nOver each finite level $(Q,L)$, each tilt $s$, and each character $\\chi\\ne 1$ of\n$\\Om_Q$, define the pathwise twisted Birkhoff functional\n\\[\nW_n(x;\\chi,s)\\;=\\;\\frac1n\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1}\\chi(x_i\\bmod Q)\\,2^{s\\Delta_{b_i}},\n\\qquad \\Delta_b=\\log_2 3-b .\n\\]\nThe family of these functionals, fibered over $\\Qcoll$, is the object; call it the\n\\emph{deterministic defect cocycle} $\\Dcoll$. At $s=0$ this is the residue Weyl\nsum: the orbit equidistributes modulo $Q$ iff $W_n(x;\\chi,0)\\to0$ for every\n$\\chi\\ne1$. The annealed pressure $M(s)$ and the critical tilt\n$p^{(1)}_b=3\\cdot4^{-b}$ are the $\\chi=\\mathbf1$, ensemble-averaged shadow of the\nsame functional, so $\\Dcoll$ is the quenched lift of the thermodynamic formalism:\n$M(s)$ is what $W_n$ self-averages to when nothing obstructs.\n\nThe content of $\\Dcoll$ at finite level is one statement in three languages---%\n\\emph{equidistribution failure}, \\emph{affine/cohomological}, and\n\\emph{spectral}:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\limsup_n \\lvert W_n(x;\\chi,s)\\rvert > 0\n\\;\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\\;&\n\\chi \\text{ is an approximate coboundary along the orbit}\\\\[2pt]\n\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\\;&\n\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}\\text{ has a near-peripheral eigenstate read off from }\\mu_n^x .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\n\\begin{remark}[Correction adopted from review]\nThe three-way \\emph{equivalence} is too strong and is used here only in the safe\ndirection. A non-decaying twisted sum signals correlation with $\\chi$, but need\nnot be an exact coboundary (it could be concentration, subsequential trapping,\nnonstationarity); and producing a near-\\emph{top} eigenstate of\n$\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}$ is precisely the open keystone, not an automatic consequence.\nThe load-bearing statement is the single implication: a persistent $W_n$ bias\n$\\Longrightarrow$ a nontrivial finite character shadow of the orbit, and\n(conjecturally) a near-peripheral state of $\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}$.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\section{Why the annealed side has a gap, explicitly}\n\nUse the exact affine cocycle\n\\[\nx_n=\\frac{3^n x_0+A_n}{2^{B_n}},\\qquad\nA_n=\\sum_{j=0}^{n-1}3^{\\,n-1-j}2^{B_j},\\qquad B_n=\\sum_{i<n}b_i .\n\\]\nReducing modulo $3^r$, for $n\\ge r$ the $3^n x_0$ term vanishes and only the last\n$r$ valuations survive.\n\n\\begin{lemma}[Sliding-window congruence]\\label{lem:cong}\nFor $n\\ge r$,\n\\[\nx_n \\equiv \\sum_{k=1}^{r} 3^{\\,k-1}\\, 2^{-(b_{n-1}+\\cdots+b_{n-k})} \\pmod{3^r}.\n\\]\n\\end{lemma}\n\\noindent Since $2$ is a primitive root modulo $3^r$ (order $2\\cdot3^{r-1}$), the\ncharacter value $\\chi_3(x_n)$ is a sliding-window function of the partial sums\n$B_i\\bmod 2\\cdot3^{r-1}$. The single-symbol Fourier transform of the annealed law\nis\n\\[\n\\hat p(\\xi)=\\sum_{b\\ge1}2^{-b}e(b\\xi)=\\frac{e(\\xi)}{2-e(\\xi)},\n\\qquad\n\\lvert\\hat p(\\xi)\\rvert=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5-4\\cos 2\\pi\\xi}}<1\\quad(\\xi\\notin\\Z).\n\\]\nThe frequencies attached to a nontrivial $\\chi$ are $\\xi=k/(2\\cdot3^{r-1})$, all\nnonintegral, so to leading order in the i.i.d.\\ model\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_{0,Q,L,\\chi})=\\max_\\xi\\lvert\\hat p(\\xi_\\chi)\\rvert<1$.\n\\epi{Inference}; this is the i.i.d.\\ value, \\emph{not} the exact spectral radius\nof the coupled finite operator. The distinction is enforced throughout\nParts~III--IV: \\texttt{rho\\_annealed} and \\texttt{rho\\_exact} are reported in\nseparate columns and never substituted.\n\n\\section{The Quenched Inverse Principle}\n\n\\begin{principlebox}{Quenched Inverse Principle (conjectural)}\nLet $x_0$ have an infinite forward orbit, and suppose it violates the annealed law\npersistently: for some $\\chi\\ne1$ on some $\\Om_Q$, some tilt $s$, and some\n$\\delta>0$, $\\lvert W_n(x_0;\\chi,s)\\rvert\\ge\\delta$ along a positive-density set of\n$n$ (or, more weakly, along blocks of length $\\ell_j\\to\\infty$). Then there exist a\nfinite pair $(Q,L)$ and a $\\chi$-sector eigenstate of $\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}$ with\n$\\lvert\\lambda\\rvert\\ge\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,0})-o(1)$, exhibited by the orbit's own\nempirical measure. Conjoined with the gap\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi})\\le(1-\\kappa_Q)\\rho(\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,0})$, this is a\ncontradiction. Hence every integer orbit equidistributes.\n\\end{principlebox}\n\n\\begin{remark}\nThe positive-density hypothesis is likely too strong: Collatz-type obstructions\nmay be sparse, clustered, or survive only across scales. The principle should\nadmit long blocks $\\ell_j\\to\\infty$ rather than positive density---the form used\nin the numerical search (Part~III, Exp.~I).\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\section{The keystone and the two leaks}\n\nThe single arrow that is not a transcription of known structure is\n\\[\n\\text{persistent quenched bias along }w(x_0)\\Longrightarrow\n\\text{a \\textbf{finite-level} approximate eigenfunction at the \\textbf{top} eigenvalue.}\n\\]\n\n\\paragraph{Finiteness leak.} The limiting obstruction could live at\n$Q\\to\\infty$. Closing this needs an effective, uniform gap\n$\\kappa_Q\\gtrsim 1/\\mathrm{polylog}(Q)$, decaying more slowly than the entropy\nproduction of the bias. That quantitative \\textsf{H23a} is a hypothesis.\n\n\\paragraph{Circularity leak.} Reading a top eigenfunction off $\\mu_n^x$ presumes\nthe orbit's residue frequencies are already near-stationary---what one is trying\nto prove. Breaking this needs an input not from the orbit's own statistics; the\nbest candidate (a regeneration-plus-bilinear engine resting on the multiplicative\nindependence of $2$ and $3$, with a Bourgain--Sarnak--Ziegler step over the\n$3$-adic frequencies) is \\epi{Speculation}, and is morally Sarnak-hard.\n\n\\section{Three falsifiable consequences (the test targets)}\n\\label{sec:targets}\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em]\n\\item For each $r$, compute the exact $\\rho(\\Lcal_{0,3^r,L,\\chi})$ and check it\ntracks $\\max_k(5-4\\cos(2\\pi k/(2\\cdot3^{r-1})))^{-1/2}$. A parametrically smaller\ncoupled gap means the finiteness leak is real and returns the true $\\kappa_Q$.\n\\item Along long orbits, compute the windowed $W_n(x;\\chi,0)$ at resonant\nfrequencies; the prediction is decay with no persistent plateau. A plateau is the\nspectral shadow made visible and a direct test of the circularity leak.\n\\item At $s=1$, compare $\\rho(\\Lcal_{1,3^r,L,\\chi})$ with\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_{1,3^r,L,0})$. A nontrivial gap there is the statement that a high\nascent cannot also hold a residue resonance.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\part{The numerical test program}\n\n\\section{What was built}\n\nThe suite \\texttt{collatz\\_tests} (ISO~C11; profiles \\textsf{FAST128} with\n\\texttt{\\_\\_uint128\\_t}, \\textsf{GMP} with \\texttt{mpz\\_t}, and a \\textsf{DEBUG}\nprofile of invariant assertions) implements nine experiments, A--I, over the\naccelerated step $S(x)=(3x+1)/2^{b}$, $b=v_2(3x+1)$. Modules cover the core\narithmetic, a streaming orbit scanner, finite characters (additive\n$\\chi_k(a)=e(ka/Q)$ and multiplicative on $(\\Z/3^r)^\\times$ with $2$ a primitive\nroot), the defect sums $W_{n,\\ell}$ via complex prefix sums, empirical\nvaluation/entropy statistics, an excursion/critical-event recorder, the finite\nresidue--height operator builder, and a CSR sparse-matrix spectral toolkit.\n\nFour design requirements, adopted from review, shaped the build:\n\\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.6em,itemsep=1pt]\n\\item the $\\bmod\\,3^r$ congruence (\\cref{lem:cong}) is treated as a\n\\emph{correctness gate} (Exp.~H), verified before any spectral code relies on it;\n\\item $\\rho_{\\mathrm{annealed}}$ and $\\rho_{\\mathrm{exact}}$ are kept in separate\ncolumns and never interchanged (Exp.~F);\n\\item the shadow search reports per-window magnitude \\emph{and} length, so both\n$\\limsup$ plateaus and long sparse blocks are visible---positive density is not\nrequired (Exp.~I);\n\\item the quenched pressure is computed as $\\limsup_n\\frac1n\\sum_{i<n}s\\Delta_{b_i}$,\nnot an ill-defined sum over words.\n\\end{enumerate}\n\n\\paragraph{A definitional caveat carried throughout.}\nThe project-internal ``$C_A$-critical'' certificate is not restated in the source\nnotes. The spec's literal inequality $V^2\\le 2^A P$ (launch $V$, peak $P$) is\n\\emph{degenerate} in practice: once the orbit descends to small record-low\nlaunches, $V^2$ is tiny and essentially every excursion qualifies. The suite\ntherefore offers two readings via \\texttt{--crit-mode}: \\texttt{vsq} (literal) and\n\\texttt{height} (peak $\\ge$ launch$\\cdot2^A$, i.e.\\ ``rises $A$ octaves''), with\n\\texttt{height} the default. Both are reported; the formal certificate should be\nfixed before the $B/D/I$ ``critical'' labels are treated as canonical.\n\n\\part{Findings}\n\nAll runs below are from a single large sweep (\\textsf{FAST128} release).\n\n\\section{Experiment H --- the congruence gate}\n\n\\begin{findingbox}{Verified}\n\\cref{lem:cong} holds in every test: \\textbf{$23{,}905{,}968/23{,}905{,}968$}\nchecks match (\\textsf{FAST128}, seeds $3$--$999{,}999$, $r\\le8$, $0$ overflow),\nplus \\textbf{$144/144$} under \\textsf{GMP} for a $16$-digit seed at $r=12$ with\n$n$ up to $155$ (values $\\gg 2^{128}$).\n\\end{findingbox}\n\\noindent The load-bearing identity is sound; downstream residue/spectral code may\nrely on it. Each check compares the directly reduced orbit value $x_n\\bmod 3^r$\nagainst the sliding-window formula using $2^{-1}\\bmod 3^r$.\n\n\\section{Experiment A --- height-tail census}\n\nPre-registration: $\\log_2\\Pr(h\\ge H)\\approx -H+C$. Over octaves $10$--$28$ at\n$10^5$ seeds/octave, the per-step slope of $\\log_2(\\text{tail}/\\text{seeds})$ at\noctave $24$ averages $\\approx\\mathbf{-0.97}$ for $H=3,\\dots,11$ (steeper in the\nlow-count tail). \\textbf{Slope $-1$ confirmed.}\n\n\\section{Experiment B --- the Cram\\'er-tilt law}\n\nPre-registration: the bulk valuation law of high-ascent excursions tends to\n$p^{(1)}_b=3\\cdot4^{-b}$ with entropy-per-digit $\\to H_2(3/4)=0.8113$.\n\n\\begin{findingbox}{Cram\\'er tilt confirmed (in direction)}\nOctave $26$, $2\\times10^5$ seeds, boundary-excluded \\emph{middle} segments of\nlength $\\ge16$ ($103{,}976$ segments):\n$\\hat p=(0.657,0.229,0.089,0.021)$---between annealed $(.5,.25,.125,.0625)$ and\ncritical $(.75,.1875,.0469,.0117)$---with\n$\\KL{\\hat p}{p^{(1)}}=\\mathbf{0.093}\\ll\\KL{\\hat p}{p}=0.191$ and\nentropy-per-digit $0.870$.\n\\end{findingbox}\n\\noindent Full segments dilute toward annealed because launch/exit boundary layers\ncontaminate them---precisely what the boundary-layer test isolates. Larger scale\nroughly doubles the margin in favor of $p^{(1)}$ over the small run.\n\n\\section{Experiment C --- defect windows}\n\nAt $s=0$ (the residue Weyl sum) $\\lvert W\\rvert$ decreases with window length\n($0.31$ at $L{=}16\\to0.24\\to0.20\\to0.11$ at $L{\\approx}116$ for $Q=27$), the\nequidistribution direction. Single-orbit windows stay short (orbits terminate at\n$1$), so the asymptotic decorrelation lives in the operator experiments E/F rather\nthan in single-orbit $W$.\n\n\\section{Experiment D --- post-exit taboo}\n\n\\begin{findingbox}{Entropy-space repulsion present}\nOctave $24$, $6{,}496$ post-exit windows: $\\KL{\\hat p_{\\rm post}}{p}=0.177\\ll\n\\KL{\\hat p_{\\rm post}}{p^{(1)}}=0.576$, mean $M_{\\rm post}(1)=0.989<1$ ($53\\%$\nsubcritical). Post-exit relaxes toward annealed, away from the critical tilt.\n\\end{findingbox}\n\\noindent This is the proposed reason two critical excursions fail to concatenate.\n\n\\section{Experiments E and F --- the finite operator gap}\n\nThe killed residue--height operator $\\Lcal_{s,Q,L,\\chi}$ is built with the forward\nresidue map $a'=(3a+1)2^{-b}$ for odd $Q$ (so $2$ is invertible, covering\n$Q=3^r$), the height update $z'=z+\\lfloor R(\\log_23-b)\\rceil$ with killing outside\n$[0,L]$, and the character phase on the source residue.\n\n\\begin{findingbox}{Target 3 (the $s=1$ gap) and Target 1 (exact vs.\\ annealed)}\nOn the reliable \\emph{residue-only} operator (Exp.~F), every nontrivial\n$3^r$-sector is gapped, $\\rho(\\Lcal_\\chi)<\\rho(\\Lcal_0)$, and the gap is\n\\emph{weakest at the critical tilt}: $s{=}1$ ratios run $0.54$--$0.93$ versus\n$0.22$--$0.76$ at $s{=}0$. Against the i.i.d.\\ Fourier prediction\n$\\lvert\\hat p(\\xi)\\rvert$, the exact ratios deviate by $\\pm0.1$--$0.36$ with\n$\\mathrm{corr}=\\mathbf{0.38}$ and mean $\\lvert\\text{dev}\\rvert=0.254$ across $48$\nsectors.\n\\end{findingbox}\n\n\\begin{remark}[A solver defect surfaced by Part~IV, since fixed]\\label{rem:Esolver}\nThe \\emph{killed-height} operator (Exp.~E) at first told a different story: its\npower iteration did not converge (relative residuals $3$--$9\\%$; all sectors hit\nthe $3000$-iteration cap), because the height-transport mode produces a\nnear-degenerate band of top eigenvalues, and several reported gap ratios\n\\emph{exceeded $1$}---impossible, since with $\\lvert\\Lcal_\\chi\\rvert$ equal to the\nprincipal operator on the same support Wielandt's theorem forces\n$\\rho(\\Lcal_\\chi)\\le\\rho(\\Lcal_0)$. The solver was replaced by a \\textbf{restarted\ncomplex Arnoldi} (Krylov subspace, Hessenberg shifted-QR Ritz values, validated\nagainst known spectra in the unit tests). The killed-height operator now converges\nto residual $<8\\times10^{-11}$ with no Wielandt violations.\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\begin{remark}[The uniform gap is governed by the \\emph{worst} character]\nA uniform gap means $\\rho(\\Lcal_\\chi)\\le(1-\\kappa_Q)\\rho(\\Lcal_0)$ for\n\\emph{all} $\\chi\\ne1$, so\n$\\kappa_Q=1-\\max_\\chi[\\rho(\\Lcal_\\chi)/\\rho(\\Lcal_{\\chi_0})]$---the\n\\emph{worst}-gapped character. (Computed over the full group via the conjugate\nsymmetry $k=1..\\lfloor\\phi/2\\rfloor$.)\n\\end{remark}\n\n\\begin{findingbox}{Target 1 (the finiteness leak), full character group}\n\\textbf{Killed-height: no uniform gap.} For Q $\\in\\{9,27,81\\}$ the maximum gap\nratio is \\emph{exactly} $1$, at the \\textbf{quadratic character} $k=\\phi/2$:\nthere $\\rho(\\Lcal_\\chi)=\\rho(\\Lcal_{\\chi_0})$ to machine precision---the\nresidue-independent height-transport survival eigenvalue ($0.9119$ at $s{=}0$,\nidentical for every $Q$). So $\\kappa_Q=0$: the height coordinate, appended\nuncoupled from the character, leaves a mode alive in the quadratic sector\n(a construction artifact, residuals $\\sim\\!0$).\n\n\\textbf{Residue-only (the object that governs $W$): a uniform gap survives.}\n$\\kappa_Q=1-\\max_\\chi$ ratio \\emph{stabilizes} as $Q$ grows---$0.236$ at\n$s{=}0$, $0.074$ at $s{=}1$ for all $Q\\in\\{27,\\dots,2187\\}$---bounded well away\nfrom $0$, with the worst character at a \\emph{fixed} frequency, not $k=1$. The\nlowest-frequency $k{=}1$ char, where the annealed $1-\\lvert\\hat p(1/\\phi)\\rvert$\ncollapses to $0$ ($0.42\\to2\\times10^{-5}$ as $Q\\to2187$), is gapped by the\n\\emph{exact} operator by $0.24$--$0.78$: the exact operator gaps low frequencies\n\\emph{far more} than the i.i.d.\\ shadow. The annealed finiteness leak is\n\\textbf{refuted} for the residue operator over $3^2\\to3^7$.\n\\end{findingbox}\n\n\\section{Experiment G --- trace and determinant shadows}\n\nFor small $(Q,L)$, $\\tr(\\Lcal^1)=0$, $\\tr(\\Lcal^2)=1.25$, $\\tr(\\Lcal^3)=0$,\n$\\tr(\\Lcal^4)=0.594$, and $\\det(1-z\\Lcal)$ is smooth and positive\n($0.95$ at $z{=}0.25\\to0.82$ at $z{=}0.5$): no spurious peripheral structure.\n\n\\section{Experiment I --- the quenched spectral-shadow search}\n\nA window is flagged shadow-bearing when $\\max_{Q,\\chi,s}\\lvert W\\rvert\\ge\\delta$\n\\emph{and} exceeds $3\\times$ a length-scaled null $\\mathrm{rms}(w)/\\sqrt{\\ell}$\n(the raw $\\delta$-only criterion saturates at $100\\%$ from short-window and\nmax-over-grid inflation). Over $1{,}254{,}232$ windows (octaves $18$--$24$,\nmoduli $\\{9,27,81,243\\}$), $4.2\\%$ flag.\n\n\\begin{center}\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.15}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{by label} & windows & frac.\\ shadow\\\\\n\\midrule\n\\texttt{critical\\_C\\_A} & $61{,}372$ & $0.084$\\\\\n\\texttt{high\\_ascent}   & $445{,}193$ & $0.054$\\\\\n\\texttt{post\\_exit}     & $747{,}621$ & $0.032$\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\hspace{2em}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{by length} & windows & frac.\\ shadow\\\\\n\\midrule\n$8$--$15$   & $331{,}996$ & $0.003$\\\\\n$16$--$31$  & $330{,}930$ & $0.029$\\\\\n$32$--$63$  & $591{,}088$ & $0.072$\\\\\n$64$--$127$ & $218$ & $\\mathbf{1.000}$\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}\n\\noindent Critical excursions carry shadows most often and post-exit least\n(taboo-consistent), and the shadow rate grows monotonically with window length,\nreaching $100\\%$ for the longest excursions. Whether this is the obstruction or a\nfinite-size effect is exactly the question of Part~IV.\n\n\\part{Relating the long shadow windows to the operator gaps}\n\nThis is the direct test of the keystone arrow: do shadow windows live in sectors\nwhere the operator gap is small? The shadow moduli $\\{9,27,81,243\\}$ are all powers\nof $3$, so multiplicative characters match between Experiments I and F, and the\njoin is on $(Q,\\chi\\text{-index},s)$. Because of \\cref{rem:Esolver} the gap is\ntaken from the residue-only operator (F), re-run over these moduli with a\n\\emph{principal-on-units} baseline ($\\chi_0=1$ on units, $0$ on non-units)---the\napples-to-apples trivial sector for multiplicative characters.\n\n\\subsection*{Tilt level: a clean correspondence}\n\n\\begin{findingbox}{Shadows sit at the weakest-gap tilt}\nF shows residue-only gaps are systematically weaker (closer to $1$) at $s{=}1$ than\nat $s{=}0$. The shadows concentrate there: of $53{,}158$ shadow windows,\n$43{,}843$ ($\\mathbf{82\\%}$) have argmax $s{=}1$, and \\textbf{all $218$ long\nwindows ($\\ell\\ge64$) are at $s{=}1$}. Long shadows also sit in weaker-gap\nsectors: mean gap ratio $\\mathbf{0.73}$ versus $0.57$ over all shadows.\n\\end{findingbox}\n\\noindent Both statements are the manuscript's predicted direction: a\nslowly-decaying mode is permitted precisely where the gap is weak, and the\ncritical tilt is where the operator gap is weakest (Target~3 made dynamical).\n\n\\subsection*{Sector level: confounded by finite residue-class count}\n\nAcross sectors at fixed tilt the correspondence is weak,\n$\\mathrm{corr}(\\lvert W\\rvert,\\text{gap})=+0.247$ (right sign, small). The argmax is\ndominated by the \\emph{smallest} modulus: $94\\%$ of shadows pick $Q=9$\n($49{,}727$ of $53{,}158$), which has only six unit residues, so a short window's\n$\\lvert\\text{mean }\\chi\\rvert$ is large by finite-sample inflation regardless of\nthe asymptotic gap (the sector $Q{=}9,k{=}3$ carries $36{,}666$ shadows yet has a\nhealthy gap of $0.60$).\n\n\\begin{keystatement}\nThe finite operator decorrelates every nontrivial sector (all residue-only gaps\n$<1$, weakest at $s=1$). The long shadow windows correspond to it in the predicted\ndirection at the level of the tilt and in gap magnitude. But the per-sector\nsingle-orbit shadow is dominated by small-$Q$ finite-size inflation, so it is\n\\emph{not} clean evidence of an operator anti-gap. This is the circularity leak\nmade concrete: reading the obstruction off one orbit's short windows is confounded;\nthe operator gap is the reliable object, and it shows no anti-gap.\n\\end{keystatement}\n\n\\part{Assessment}\n\n\\section*{Where the program stands}\n\\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Assessment}\n\n\\begin{center}\n\\renewcommand{\\arraystretch}{1.3}\n\\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.40\\linewidth} p{0.52\\linewidth}@{}}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Component} & \\textbf{Status after testing}\\\\\n\\midrule\nCongruence \\cref{lem:cong} & verified, $2.4\\times10^7$ checks \\epi{Confirmed}\\\\\nannealed gap $\\lvert\\hat p(\\xi)\\rvert<1$ & realized; exact operator deviates from it \\epi{Refined}\\\\\nCram\\'er tilt on critical excursions & bulk leans to $p^{(1)}$, $\\KL{\\cdot}{p^{(1)}}\\ll\\KL{\\cdot}{p}$ \\epi{Confirmed}\\\\\npost-exit taboo & $M_{\\rm post}(1)<1$, repelled from $p^{(1)}$ \\epi{Confirmed}\\\\\nfinite character-sector gap (Target 3) & present, weakest at $s=1$ (residue-only) \\epi{Confirmed}\\\\\nresidue-only uniform $\\kappa_Q$ (Target 1) & stabilizes $\\to0.236$ ($s{=}0$)/$0.074$ ($s{=}1$) over $3^2\\!\\to\\!3^7$; leak refuted \\epi{Confirmed}\\\\\nkilled-height uniform $\\kappa_Q$ & $=0$: quadratic char carries the height mode (construction artifact) \\epi{Flagged}\\\\\nshadow $\\Rightarrow$ small operator gap & holds by tilt; per-sector confounded by $Q$ \\epi{Partial}\\\\\nQuenched Inverse Principle / keystone & untouched; circularity leak made concrete \\epi{Open}\\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\end{center}\n\n\\paragraph{Immediate follow-ups.}\n(1)~The killed-height solver is now a converged Arnoldi; extend $\\kappa_Q$ to the\n\\emph{full} character group (not the first four) and push $Q$ higher to test\nwhether $\\kappa_Q\\gtrsim1/\\mathrm{polylog}(Q)$ survives as $Q\\to\\infty$ (the\nfiniteness leak). (2)~Switch the defect-window\nexperiment to multiplicative characters for $3^r$ and measure per-sector\n$\\lvert W_{n,\\ell}\\rvert$ decay against the F gap rate at fixed $(Q,\\chi,s)$,\ncontrolling for $Q$. (3)~Normalize the shadow detector by residue-class count so\nthe per-sector signal is not swamped by small $Q$. (4)~Fix the formal\n$C_A$-critical certificate so the $B/D/I$ critical labels are canonical.\n\nThe object relocates the difficulty rather than removing it. The experiments show\n$\\Dcoll$ behaves as advertised on the parts that are reorganizations of known\nstructure---the congruence, the annealed gap, the Cram\\'er tilt, the post-exit\ntaboo, and a genuine finite operator gap weakest at the critical tilt---while the\none open arrow (a persistent quenched bias forcing a top finite-level eigenstate)\nremains open, and the single-orbit shadow that would witness it is confounded by\nfinite size exactly as the circularity leak predicts.\n\n\\end{document}\n","replaceAll":false,"structuredPatch":[{"lines":[" \\end{center}"," "," \\paragraph{Immediate follow-ups.}","-(1)~The killed-height solver is now a converged Arnoldi; extend $\\kappa_Q$ to the","-\\emph{full} character group (not the first four) and push $Q$ higher to test","-whether $\\kappa_Q\\gtrsim1/\\mathrm{polylog}(Q)$ survives as $Q\\to\\infty$ (the","-finiteness leak). (2)~Switch the defect-window","+(1)~The residue-only uniform gap is now computed over the full group to $Q=3^7$","+and stabilizes; the open structural question is the killed-height","+\\emph{quadratic-character} degeneracy ($\\kappa_Q=0$): rebuild the height","+coordinate \\emph{coupled} to the character (or prove the quadratic mode is a","+genuine height-transport artifact) so the killed operator inherits the residue","+gap. (2)~Switch the defect-window"," experiment to multiplicative characters for $3^r$ and measure per-sector"," $\\lvert W_{n,\\ell}\\rvert$ decay against the F gap rate at fixed $(Q,\\chi,s)$,"," controlling for $Q$. (3)~Normalize the shadow detector by residue-class count so"],"newLines":12,"newStart":517,"oldLines":10,"oldStart":517}],"userModified":false}